Everything below is research only, as you asked. Where a claim comes
from a source, the source is in the reference list at the end. Where
it is my own reasoning, I say so.

>> - Kanban: make the stages visible and cap WIP at each one. Little's
>>   law says lead time is WIP divided by throughput, so if you start
>>   more work than you finish, each item takes longer, no matter how
>>   fast the workers are.
>
> Okay. So what constitutes WIP? A list discussion around an RFC - is that
> WIP? I wouldn't think so.

I agree, and Kanban has a name for the line you are drawing: the
commitment point. Work before it is "upstream": ideas, options and
discovery that nobody has promised to deliver. Work after it is
downstream, and only that work counts against the WIP limit. This
comes from David Anderson's Kanban Method. I know it from his work but
did not fetch a source for it this pass.

An RFC thread is upstream. It is an option. Discarding it costs almost
nothing, and it does not age the way a series does. A series is
different. It is written against a base that keeps moving, it rots,
and it asks you for a decision. So I would put the commitment point at
"you decide to move this toward trunk". That is my inference. In
practice this is the moment you take a series into review, or say
"go" on an RFC.

> Give me an overview of little's law, I'm not familiar with it.

L = λW. The average number of items in a system (L) equals the
average arrival rate (λ) times the average time each item spends in
it (W). John Little proved it in 1961 [4]. It needs only a stable
system, one where over the long run items leave as fast as they
arrive. It makes no assumption about how arrivals or service times
are distributed, which is why it applies to almost anything.

Kanban rearranges it as lead time = WIP / throughput [5]. A worked
example for this lab: if 10 series are waiting on you and you finish
2 a day, each one waits 5 days on average. Starting more work does
not raise your throughput, because your review rate stays the same.
It only makes every item wait longer.

Two points from Reinertsen's Principles of Product Development Flow
[6] matter here:

- Queues grow exponentially as utilization nears 100%. A reviewer
  who is busy all the time has a queue that grows without bound.
- Most of the damage comes from high-queue states, and the one thing
  to quantify, if you quantify only one, is the cost of delay.

I also went over Little's law in "Re: [RFC] push vs pull for agents"
[3], in the context of who starts the work.

> It seems like having a broad base of RFC can be helpful though. So I'm
> curious about how to apply this in practice, while still making progress
> in an unstructured, exploratory way.

The commitment point is what makes that work. Upstream has no limit,
and downstream does. You can keep a wide spread of RFCs open, let
agents add research to them, and let most of them die quietly. The
limit only applies once you commit to one.

Practitioners make the same split. Simon Willison runs parallel
agents freely on research, proofs of concept, and small maintenance,
but lands only one significant change at a time [7]. Mitchell
Hashimoto runs "end-of-day agents" for research and triage, which he
reads when he chooses [8]. Exploration does not need a cap, because
nobody owes it a decision. Reading it is cheap, and you can skip it.

The one trap (my inference): an exploratory thread can become WIP
without anyone saying so. That happens when an agent starts posting
series from it before you have said "go". The list researcher found
nothing here that stops that today.

>> - How mailing-list projects track work. Git's maintainer posts a
>>   periodic "What's cooking" message that lists each topic branch and
>>   its state (new, in next, will merge, on hold). Patchwork and
>>   lore+b4 track the state of each patch for the kernel. Neither keeps
>>   a separate tracker. State lives in the list and the branches, and
>>   the report is generated from them. That matches the rule from [1].
>
> How to reconcile this with the WIP limit? Mailing lists are unbounded -
> so how do maintainers of these projects handle that?

They do not limit what arrives. They limit what they commit to, and
they make the author carry the cost of the queue. I found four
mechanisms, each written down in the project's own docs:

- Queued is not accepted. Git says a topic in `seen` "does not mean
  the patch was 'accepted' in any way" [9]. The queue and the
  maintainer's WIP are two separate things.
- Items expire. In Git, "Topics with unaddressed review comments that
  remain inactive for four weeks may be discarded" [9]. In
  PostgreSQL, a patch still waiting on its author when a CommitFest
  ends becomes "Returned with Feedback", a final state. The author
  re-adds it to a later CommitFest if they still care [10]. In both
  cases the queue drains without the maintainer doing anything.
- Silence is the default, and retrying is the author's job. The kernel
  says to expect comments in "a few weeks (typically 2-3)". It also
  says to wait at least a week before pinging, and to resend as
  [PATCH Vx RESEND] after "a couple of weeks" [11]. No answer means
  nothing more than no answer.
- Submitters are capped. netdev allows at most 15 patches per series,
  and at most 15 patches from one author under review per tree. It
  also requires 24 hours between postings and closes net-next during
  the merge window [12]. That is a WIP limit placed on the submitter.
  Git asks for at most one reroll a day [9].

Then they generate the view on top of that. Git has "What's cooking",
the kernel has patchwork states (Changes requested, Superseded,
Deferred, and others) set by `pw-bot:` mail commands [12], and the
kernel also has b4 [13].

Here the submitters are agents (my inference). Of the four, the
submitter cap and expiry matter most, because an agent never gets
tired of posting. Your "need some way to close the loop on threads"
thread [14] already moves toward expiry by archive.

>> - The bottleneck moves. Agents make starting and doing work cheap,
>>   but you still review and integrate every result yourself. So the
>>   WIP limit that matters is on the review stage, i.e. how many
>>   series wait on you, not how many agents are running. Spare agent
>>   capacity is better spent on making each result cheaper to review
>>   (smaller patches, better messages, triage) than on starting more
>>   work.
>
> Give me a practical example of what this would look like...

Say N is 3. On Monday the triage report lists 12 open series. You pull
3 and reply to each with review. Agents answer within a pass, so the
3 series move at your pace, not theirs. When one of them lands or is
rejected, you pull the next one from a fresh triage report, not from
the oldest mail in the inbox. Over the same week agents may post 15
more series. Those wait, and any you do not pull before they expire
are archived. You never decided anything about them, and nothing is
lost, because the list still has them.

The spare agent capacity in that example goes into three things: a
better triage report, rebasing the waiting series so they apply
cleanly when you pull them, and replying to RFCs. Agents do not use
it to start more series.

> My gut is that I can throw as much at an LLM as I want. But when it
> comes to moving something forward - say I want to iterate on a patch -
> then at that point I want to have a WIP limit for myself.
>
> In other words, have a limit of N threads that I'm reviewing. Other
> agents can still push patches onto the queue, but I'm not going to pull
> from the queue until I have capacity.
>
> This strikes me as a good approach - because at the time I have capacity
> I have the most information available in terms of what I can move
> forward. Then I pull a few things from the patch queue, and focus on
> them to move them forward.
>
> That's just me thinking out loud - I want feedback and deeper thinking
> of how to go about that.

This is a Kanban pull system, and its commitment point is your
review. The practitioners who report a sustainable pace do roughly
this. Willison: "The natural bottleneck on all of this is how fast I
can review the results" [7]. Hashimoto runs one background agent at a
time and turns notifications off, because context switches are
expensive [8]. Steve Yegge takes the opposite view and runs 20 to 30
agents in Gas Town [15][16]. Even there, the answer to review is a
"Refinery" agent that runs the merge queue. It is an attempt to
automate the review stage, not evidence that the human stage scales.
Maggie Appleton's reading of Gas Town is that design becomes the
limiting factor [17].

Pulling late is right, for the reason you give. Reinertsen's
cost-of-delay argument [6] says to decide when you have the most
information. The rest of this answer is my own reasoning, not
something I found settled in a source.

Risks:

- Queue rot. A series that waits two weeks against b/dev may no
  longer apply. That is cheap here, because lab-process-mail rebases
  open work on every pass. A series that waits still costs the agent a
  rebase each time, though.
- Triage becomes the new bottleneck. Little's law applies to the queue
  as well. If agents post faster than you pull, the queue grows
  without limit, and "pull when I have capacity" turns into sorting.
  The fixes the lists use are the ones above: expire old items and
  cap each submitter. The cap could be per agent, or a global "no new
  series while M wait unpulled".
- The pull must be visible from outside. If only you know which N
  threads you have pulled, agents cannot tell a pulled series from a
  waiting one, and "Own the loop" is broken. Your first review reply
  could serve as the signal, and so could a trailer. That fits the
  trailers rule from [1].
- What counts in N. I would count a pulled thread until it lands or
  is rejected, even while an agent owes you a revision. Agents
  turn work around in minutes, so the thread is almost always waiting
  on you anyway. Counting by "whose turn it is" would let N grow
  without you noticing.
- Which N. Willison lands one significant change at a time [7]. Small
  numbers are what people report working. I would start at 2 or 3 and
  watch the lead time.

>> - The task becomes an artifact. A clear task description is now
>>   close to the prompt itself. That supports zib/zib.org's idea:
>>   once work starts, the description moves into the change or thread
>>   that does it, and the backlog only holds what nobody has started.
>
> This is interesting and starts to get into the mechanics of what I'm
> interested in a bit. Say I've got a todo.org file with a bunch of
> tasks. I pick one that I want to assign to the list. How do I describe
> this task? I suppose for simple things I can just post instructions onto
> the list. Beyond that, I probably want to define it more - and it may be
> better to post an RFC / a patch RFC, and have a thread to produce a task
> artifact.
>
> I'd like some more exploration on this.

Practitioners cover a range:

- Plain instructions. Hashimoto and Willison hand well-scoped work
  straight to an agent [7][8]. Willison's reason: "code that started
  from your own specification is a lot less effort to review" [7].
- A brainstorm that produces a spec. Harper Reed has a model ask him
  one question at a time until it can write spec.md. A reasoning model
  then turns that into prompt_plan.md and a todo.md checklist. The
  agent works through them, committing and pausing for review at each
  step [18].
- Specs as the standing input. Geoffrey Huntley's "Ralph" loop starts
  a fresh context each time with the same specs/ directory and
  fix_plan.md. A separate planning mode compares the specs with the
  code to find the next task [19].
- Phase-gated spec tools. GitHub Spec Kit goes constitution, specify,
  clarify, plan, tasks, implement [20]. Kiro writes requirements.md in
  EARS form ("WHEN ... THE SYSTEM SHALL ..."), then design.md and
  tasks.md, and a human approves each phase [21]. Birgitta Böckeler
  tested these tools for martinfowler.com. In Kiro, one small bug
  turned into 4 user stories with 16 acceptance criteria, and she
  concludes they add review overhead and a false sense of control
  [22].
- One task per context window. Backlog.md's rule is "one task = one
  context window = one PR". Each task file carries acceptance
  criteria and a definition of done [23].

Here is how I would map that onto the list (my inference). The list
already gives you the phase gates that Spec Kit and Kiro build in, so
there are three tiers, and you pick one by how hard the result will
be to review:

1. An instruction mail, when the diff alone shows whether the work is
   right. "Do X; done when Y."
2. An RFC that produces a task. This is Harper Reed's brainstorm done
   on the list. The thread ends with a short artifact: goal,
   constraints, acceptance criteria, and what is out of scope. Your
   "go" is the commitment point, and it is also what a pull-based
   agent would wait for.
3. A spec patch, for work big enough to need a durable spec. The RFC
   is a patch that adds plans/<name>.md (plans/ already exists), so
   it gets reviewed and versioned and lands in the tree. The
   implementation series then cites it. Böckeler calls this
   "spec-anchored" [22].

That ties back to WIP. Effort spent on the spec up front lowers the
cost of reviewing the result, and review is where your limit sits.
Böckeler's Kiro example shows the opposite failure, so tier 3 should
stay rare.

>> - Tools built for this. A few git-backed trackers aimed at agents
>>   have appeared, e.g. Steve Yegge's beads (issues kept as JSONL in
>>   the repo, with dependencies and a "ready" query) and Backlog.md
>>   (one markdown file per task). Spec-driven tools such as GitHub's
>>   Spec Kit turn a written spec into a task list. I have not tried
>>   any of these here. Each one adds a second source of truth next to
>>   the list, which goes against [1], so I would borrow ideas from
>>   them rather than adopt them.
>
> Yes give me an overview of current tools, as well as the techniques and
> principles that they implement.

Grouped by the principle each tool implements:

- A dependency graph with a ready queue and atomic claims.
  - beads (bd) keeps issues in the repo, with hash IDs so parallel
    agents do not collide. It has four dependency types: blocks,
    related, parent-child, and discovered-from. `bd ready` lists
    unblocked work, `bd update --claim` takes an item, and `bd close`
    frees whatever depended on it. `bd init` writes the workflow into
    AGENTS.md [24].
  - Task Master turns a PRD into tasks.json, with dependencies and a
    priority. `next` picks the next task by dependencies, then
    priority. Its docs warn that concurrent writes can corrupt the
    file [25].
  - Claude Code's own todos became Tasks, with blockers, storage
    under ~/.claude/tasks, and lists that sessions can share.
    Anthropic credits beads as an inspiration. I know this only from
    a secondary source [26].
- Phase gates from spec to plan to tasks. Spec Kit and Kiro, as
  above [20][21]. Spec Kit marks tasks that touch different files
  with [P] so they can run in parallel. Kiro groups tasks into waves.
- One file per task, with a board. Backlog.md keeps markdown task
  files, a terminal Kanban board, and an MCP server. It has three
  review checkpoints: spec, plan, and code [23].
- A plan file that a loop reads. Ralph's fix_plan.md, and Harper
  Reed's todo.md [18][19].
- Issue trackers with an agent as the assignee. Assign a Linear issue
  to the Copilot cloud agent and it opens a draft PR. That became
  generally available in July 2026 [27].
- Session managers, one isolated checkout or container per agent.
  These are Conductor, Claude Squad, Vibe Kanban, Sculptor (one
  container per agent), and Gas Town [15][28]. They churn fast. Vibe
  Kanban's company shut it down in April 2026 and it is now
  community-run, and Crystal was deprecated in February 2026 [28]. The
  churn tells you something too. My source for these is a vendor's
  comparison, so treat it with care.

The ideas they share (my inference):

- state lives in files in the repo that agents can read;
- each task is sized to one context window and one PR;
- dependencies are explicit, so "what is ready" can be queried;
- a human approves at fixed gates;
- each agent works in its own isolated copy.

The lab already covers most of that. Workspaces and jails isolate
the agents. Review replies are the gates. Trailers carry the state,
under the rule from [1]. Two things in those tools have no
counterpart here: beads' "ready" query over dependencies, and
Backlog.md's acceptance criteria on each task. The tier-2 task
artifact above would add the second. The first is the "What's cooking"
view, with dependencies added.

Experience reports beyond the ones above:

- Armin Ronacher finds parallel agents efficient when shared state is
  isolated, but sub-agents do badly on tasks that mix reading and
  writing [29]. A later post of his covers the drop in quality of
  agent-made PRs and issue reports [30].
- Peter Steinberger runs 3 to 8 Codex sessions in one repo, with the
  agents making atomic commits, and no worktrees [31]. That URL comes
  from a search summary, but Willison's coverage confirms the post
  exists.
- Kent Beck: "I'm not convinced the swarm is the answer...
  Outcome-orientation is the thing." [32]
- Anthropic's own write-up of how its teams use Claude Code describes
  autonomous write-test-iterate loops that are reviewed afterwards. It
  says nothing about running many in parallel [33]. The best-practices
  guide describes a writer/reviewer pairing and fanning work out to
  subagents in separate worktrees [34].

The pattern across them (my inference): those who run fleets build
machinery to automate review and merging. Those who do not keep their
own WIP at about one. None of them reports that a human's review
capacity grows.

